be alternate: 2026: 0.001, 2030: 0.02, 2035: 0.10, 2040: 0.25, 2050: 0.55. Which better? Need \"Use existing knowledge\" but we as model trained maybe not 2026 events. Forecast from 2026. There may already be solution? Since prompt says at forecast date: at least one open; we assume no qualifying solution in public yet maybe? It doesn't say state of world beyond existing knowledge. Use existing knowledge; likely no solution by mid-2026. Need info cutoff? We don't know 2026 events. We should estimate from pre-training? But as AI in 2026, likely no solution. Maybe 2026 0.001 or 0.003. For consistency, if no known solution at forecast, event by end of 2026 tiny.\n\nConsider probability of AI proof in 4.5 months: AI might produce a complete solution with some proof step that's actually correct? If random frontier model, extremely low. But maybe current AI could produce complete solutions to known hard problems? For millennium, no. A model might hallucinate plausible false proof; misidentified as correct? Event requires correct. Need fully complete solution. Very unlikely. 1e-5? But maybe event: first publicly available with AI contributed, not known to be correct yet; later validation could establish. A preprint by end year with AI assistant could be plausible if a human has close proof? Maybe 0.005.\n\nUse a step function: 0.005 0.04 0.14 0.32 0.68. Let's ensure JSON keys strings. \n\nCould the existence of AI assistance lower threshold further: Any AI proof step by 2030? Hmm.\n\nLet's examine historical rate for Millennium problems: Since 2000, no new; many candidates. Could a problem have been close for years? Some claims: P = NP? false. Riemann hypothesis many papers false. The probability of solution by 2050 among all open math problems? Hard. There is base possibility from competition: 6 problems, over 25 years with large talent and AI; maybe 30%. Need account AI could make lucky search. If AI can run massive random exploration with proof verification, perhaps hit